WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
From a board record to a board you can build: six steps with the real files.

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.800
Step 1, find a board. Query the 322 records by format, licence or vendor: 25 boards have a verified STEP model, 234 records allow commercial reuse.

3
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:16.200
Step 2, fetch one board. A sparse clone of the SparkFun IoT RedBoard RP2350 takes 10 seconds and 5.3 MB instead of 678 MB, and shasum matches its ATTRIBUTION.md.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:22.100
Step 3, open the design in KiCad. The RedBoard files are KiCad 8 projects, and Eagle files import into KiCad as well.

5
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:28.000
Step 4, send the Gerbers to a board house. The SparkFun Edge archive holds copper, solder mask, silkscreen and paste for each side, the outline and the drill file.

6
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.400
Step 5, design the enclosure around the STEP model. The BeagleBone AI-64 measures 102.5 by 82.6 by 36.6 millimetres, heatsink and connectors included.

7
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:39.800
Step 6, keep the credit and the licence. CC BY-SA, 212 boards: credit and share alike. CC BY 4.0, 12 boards: credit. MIT, BSD and Apache-2.0, 7 boards: keep the licence text. One grant in 0BSD wording: no conditions.

8
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:45.300
583 CAD files from 232 boards, every one credited. embeddedos.org/open-hardware.
